    <td width="95%" class="titlebg">&nbsp;General Discussion / Anna's Column / <a href="http://www.huntri.com/cgi-bin/yabb2/YaBB.pl?num=1144025049/0#0"><u>OPENING DAY ALMOST HERE! ARE YOU READY?</u></a><br />
    &nbsp;<span class="small">on: 04/02/06 at 20:44:08&nbsp;</span></td>
  </tr><tr>
    <td colspan="2" class="catbg">Started by <a href="http://www.huntri.com/cgi-bin/yabb2/YaBB.pl?action=viewprofile;username=trouttrigger" rel="nofollow">Anna Minicucci</a> | Post by <a href="http://www.huntri.com/cgi-bin/yabb2/YaBB.pl?action=viewprofile;username=trouttrigger" rel="nofollow">Anna Minicucci</a></td>
  </tr><tr>
    <td colspan="2" class="windowbg2" valign="top">FISHING FOLKS ARE YOU READY? OPENING DAY ALMOST HERE! <br /> &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp; &nbsp;One of life’s pleasures, consistent for Rhode Island fishing folks, is Opening Day of Freshwater Fishing (rout season). Saturday, April 8th is the day, 6:00 a.m, is the time to throw the first cast. <br /> &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp; &nbsp;As usual, true to the longtime, established, second Saturday in April, Opening Day has become a tradition likened to a holiday, and it’s one, even though weather may not be cooperative. It has never dampened Opening Day spirits, excitement, and anticipation. <br /> &nbsp; <br /> &nbsp; &nbsp;Just what is it about this day which drives family and friends to gather along rivers, streams, lakes, and ponds? Undaunted by an early morning chill, early, and overnight trout fishers revel in vying for who will catch the first trout within their group. Using new, or seasoned, fishing gear, new lures, or freshly bought ‘crawlers(the best live bait) brings anticipation to a greater level. &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp; &nbsp;Fishermen and women usually stake out favorite fishing places in advance, an experience, which has been going on for years. Bait &amp; tackle shops are busy places but the best to visit before Opening Day. Sales, &nbsp;free hot coffee and snacks are usually available; however, more important, are the tips and suggestions for what to use, how-to, and where to go for successful results. Support of these (very) small businesses, by anglers, is rewarding, as are web sites featuring advice and fishing tales (see the end of this colum for website addresses.). <br /> &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp; &nbsp;All one need do to take in the “flavor” of Opening Day, is to observe Meadow Brook Pond, Wyoming Pond, Carbuncle Pond, Wood River, Carolina Trout Pond, Olney Pond, Round Top Pond, the Blackstone River, Alton Pond, and the Pawcatuck River. These are but a few of more than 80 trout stocked sites where shorelines are rimmed with youngsters and adults exerting casts to reach a possible trout catch. <br /> &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp; Ponds and rivers are dotted with small watercraft carrying fisher folks. Anglers wading streamside and the “back forty” of lakes and ponds cast thoughts of chilly air and cold waters behind them as they warm up to Opening Day first casts. It’s a time when spin fishers and fly fishing folks are shoulder-to-shoulder, whether they like it or not. There are fly fishing only trout stocked sites listed in the Freshwater Fishing Abstract, or onlie. <br /> &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp; &nbsp;All this and more make up the “Holiday festivities” of Opening Day for those who want to catch and keep one or two of the 70,000 trout which have been stocked by DEM’s Division of Fish and Wildlife. To keep their catch anglers must purchase a Trout Stamp, and place it on their fishing license. <br /> &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp; &nbsp;The “reward” is hanging one of the two year old brown, brook, and rainbow trout which have an estimated, average, weight &nbsp;of one pound. Additionally, 10 percent of the stocked trout are two pound brown and rainbow trout. The stocking program takes place in more than 80 ponds, streams, and rivers throughout the state. <br /> &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp; &nbsp;An exception to the stocking program will be Biscuit City Pond in Kingston which remains off the stocking list at the request of the Biscuit City Neighborhood Association, who own the area. <br /> &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp; &nbsp;A GOOD THING: ADDITIONS TO CHILDREN’S FISHING AREAS <br /> &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp; &nbsp;The Division stocks six ponds restricted to youngster 14 years and younger. Add to that, Cass Pond in Woonsocket, and Slater Pond in Pawtucket. These two ponds will be open for children only during the first two days of freshwater fishing season. <br /> &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp; &nbsp;Parents and other adults, should consider, the continuing “First Fish’ program for youngsters. Children, with the help of an adult, may fill out an internet online certificate which will be signed by the Chief of Fish and Wildlife. Additional “First Fish” info may be found at: <a href="http://www.dem.ri.gov" target="_blank">www.dem.ri.gov</a>. Click on “Programs” then on Fish and Wildlife, and then go to Freshwater Fisheries. It would seem the online route to the information is somewhat akin to to a “fishing trip.” That’s okay, it’s a good exercise in finding and locating the “Fish Program.” Hooking it and receiving it is a certificate worthy of framing. <br /> &nbsp;<br />  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; A FEW ANGLING RULES <br /> &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp; &nbsp;Fishing regulations are not complicated and are easily understandable. <br /> &nbsp; <br /> When and if, approached by a DEM Environmental Officer an angler must have their fishing license and Trout Stamp visible. Everything after that may be violations of the number of trout kept. Follow the regs to enjoy and have a fun fishing time. <br /> &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp; &nbsp;Everyone, 15 years and older, are required to purchase a fishing license ( $18.00 residents and Armed Forces members), if they want to catch fish. The Trout Conservation Stamp is another requirement for anyone wishing to keep or possess trout, or to fish at any “catch-and-release” or “fly fishing only” areas. Combination hunting and fishing license ($33.00 for residents, $35.00, nonresidents.) A consecutive three-day, nonresident, tourist license, $16.00. Anglers over 65 must have a license, which is free, but need not purchase a <br /> Trout Stamp. <br /> &nbsp;<br /> &nbsp; Finally, Opening Day of Freshwater Fishing (trout season) has always been a family and friends event. Today, there are complaints about families and a lack of cohesiveness. This has never been true of those who fish. Opening Day offers an opportunity for families and friends to fish and be together in a outdoor setting where the activity is rewarding in many ways. One, is creating memories. A noble reward for all involved. <br /> </td>
  </tr><tr>
